<article class="env-lemma" id="0CUA">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0CUA">Lemma <span data-tag="0CUA">71.3.4</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $f : X \to Y$ be a morphism of algebraic spaces over $S$. Let $\mathcal{G}$ be a quasi-coherent $\mathcal{O}_ Y$-module. Let $x \in |X|$ and $y = f(x) \in |Y|$. If </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$y \in \text{WeakAss}_ S(\mathcal{G})$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$f$ is flat at $x$, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
the dimension of the local ring of the fibre of $f$ at $x$ is zero (Morphisms of Spaces, Definition <a href="/tag/04NM" data-tag="04NM">67.33.1</a>), 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> then $x \in \text{WeakAss}(f^*\mathcal{G})$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Choose a scheme $V$, a point $v \in V$, and an étale morphism $V \to Y$ mapping $v$ to $y$. Choose a scheme $U$, a point $u \in U$, and an étale morphism $U \to V \times _ Y X$ mapping $v$ to a point lying over $v$ and $x$. This is possible because there is a $t \in |V \times _ Y X|$ mapping to $(v, y)$ by Properties of Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/03H4" data-tag="03H4">66.4.3</a>. By definition we see that the dimension of $\mathcal{O}_{U_ v, u}$ is zero. Hence $u$ is a generic point of the fiber $U_ v$. By our definition of weakly associated points the problem is reduced to the morphism of schemes $U \to V$. This case is treated in Divisors, Lemma <a href="/tag/05F0" data-tag="05F0">31.6.4</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>